1wjv|a|Firm; stiff; unyielding; not pliant; not flexible. ; Hence, not lax or indulgent; severe; inflexible; strict; as, a rigid father or master; rigid discipline; rigid criticism; a rigid sentence.	j:hnv,109,3;15p,98,4;vt8,64,7;95j,62,9;vjf,57,4;252a,53,7;1xbe,53,13;vj7,46,6;1ccj,41,9;1xba,37,3;u7p,32,3;2020,24,6;2fwy,5,4~1xbe,53,13;95j,62,9;1ccj,41,9;252a,53,7;vt8,64,7;vj7,46,6;2020,24,6;vjf,57,4;2fwy,5,4;15p,98,4;hnv,109,3;u7p,32,3;1xba,37,3	e:1k3x,70,6;tb2,55,10;t25,48,7;2epf,38,47;29qk,35,17;19lo,31,9;1qqa,7,1;1f74,4,8~2epf,38,47;29qk,35,17;tb2,55,10;19lo,31,9;1f74,4,8;t25,48,7;1k3x,70,6;1qqa,7,1
1wjw|n|The quality or state of being rigid; want of pliability; the quality of resisting change of form; the amount of resistance with which a body opposes change of form; -- opposed to flexibility, ductility, malleability, and softness. ; Stiffness of appearance or manner; want of ease or elegance. ; Severity; rigor.	a:1f74,25,7;1fiq,10,1~1f74,25,7;1fiq,10,1
